@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli Command Line Options
@brief Options for controlling test output verbosity at run-time.

@details
The µTest++ framework offers flexible control over test-output verbosity,
allowing users to tailor the level of detail to their specific
requirements and workflow.

By default, the reporter presents detailed results only for failed test cases, while successful test cases are summarised in a single line, indicating the total number of passed and failed checks.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test one two --reporter=tap

TAP version 14
# Subtest: Minimal

    # Subtest: Check various conditions
        1..0
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # { passed, 2 checks }

    1..1
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

@section cli-reporter Reporter

By default, the µTest++ output follows the [TAP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_Anything_Protocol) rules.

For compatibility reasons, the proprietary format implemented by the initial
releases will also be retained, but it is not recommended for new projects.

If needed, more reporting formats (like JUnit, JSON, etc.) can be added.

To explicitly ask for a specific format:

- `--reporter=tap` - the default TAP reporter
- `--reporter=human` - the human-readable reporter

@section cli-verbosity Verbosity

By default, µTest++ displays a summary line for each passing test case and full expectation details for each failing one.

To adjust the verbosity, the following command-line options are available:

- `--verbose` – display all expectations, regardless of outcome
- `--quiet` – display only the session totals and, with the TAP reporter,
  one result line per test suite; the failed checks are not displayed
- `--silent` – suppress all output and return only the exit code

These options enable developers to focus on the information most relevant to their needs, whether that is comprehensive diagnostics during development or succinct summaries for automated builds.

The examples provided illustrate the output produced in each mode, demonstrating how µTest++ adapts its reporting to support professional and efficient testing workflows across all files and folders.

The source code for this example is in
`tests/sources/minimal-test/src/main.cpp`.

@subsection cli-verbosity-verbose Verbose mode

In verbose mode, the µTest++ reporter provides detailed output for every
expectation, irrespective of whether it passes or fails.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test one two --verbose --reporter=tap

TAP version 14
# Subtest: Minimal

    # Subtest: Check various conditions
        ok 1 - answer is 42
        ok 2 - condition is true
        1..2
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # { passed, 2 checks }

    1..1
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

@subsection cli-verbosity-quiet Quiet mode

In quiet mode, the µTest++ reporter displays only the overall session
totals. The TAP reporter also displays one `ok`/`not ok` line per test
suite, with the plan counting them, so that the output remains a valid
TAP document, which the TAP consumers read as failed when a suite
failed.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test one two --quiet --reporter=tap

TAP version 14
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }
1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

Quiet mode tells whether the run failed, not what failed: the failed
checks, their locations, and their test cases are not displayed. This is
by design, since displaying in full only the failed test cases is what
the default mode does. To learn what failed, run without `--quiet`, or
keep `--quiet` and add `--output-file` with the TAP reporter, whose file
always receives the complete report, whatever the verbosity:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --quiet --output-file=sample-test.tap
@endcode

With the human reporter, the output file is complete only in the default
and verbose modes, so in quiet mode neither the console nor the file
shows the failures.

When exceptions are not enabled and a failed requirement aborts the test
execution, the results of the aborted test case are displayed in quiet
mode as well, since the totals are never reached.

@subsection cli-verbosity-silent Silent mode

In silent mode, the µTest++ reporter suppresses all output, and the
framework returns only the exit code.

@section cli-filter Filter

By default, all test suites and test cases are run. To run only some of
them, the `--filter` option selects them by name, as `--grep` does in
node-tap:

- `--filter=<pattern>` or `--filter <pattern>` – run only the suites and
  the test cases whose names match the patterns

The option may be repeated, one pattern per nesting level: the first
pattern applies to the suite names, the second to the names of the test
cases, and each further pattern to the names of the test cases nested one
level deeper. The levels without a pattern are not filtered; to filter
only the test cases, use `*` for the suites.

In a pattern, `*` matches any sequence of characters, including an empty
one, `?` matches any single character, and `|` separates alternatives. The
match is case-sensitive, and the pattern must cover the whole name; for
example, `Args` does not select a suite named `Args suite`, but `Args*`
and `Args*|*namespace*` do. Since the patterns usually contain wildcards,
they must be quoted, to prevent the shell from expanding them.

The suites and the test cases that are not selected are reported as
skipped, keep their numbers in the TAP plans, and their code is not run.
The top suite is never skipped as a whole, since its body is `main()`, but
its test cases are filtered like those of any other suite.

On platforms without a command line, the patterns can be set in
`runner_options::filter_patterns`.

@par Example

@code{.console}
$ sample-test one two --filter='*namespace*'

TAP version 14
# Subtest: Sample
...
ok 1 - Sample # { passed, 31 checks in 13 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

ok 2 - Args suite # SKIP filtered

# Subtest: Explicit namespace suite
...
ok 3 - Explicit namespace suite # { passed, 2 checks in 2 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

# Subtest: Implicit namespace suite
...
ok 4 - Implicit namespace suite # { passed, 2 checks in 2 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

1..4
# { total: 35 checks passed, 0 failed, in 17 test cases, 4 test suites, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

To run only the test cases named `Check one` of the same suites:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test one two --filter='*namespace*' --filter='Check one'
...
# Subtest: Explicit namespace suite

    # Subtest: Check one
        1..0
    ok 1 - Check one # { passed, 1 check }

    ok 2 - Check two # SKIP filtered

    1..2
ok 3 - Explicit namespace suite # { passed, 1 check in 1 test case, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
...
1..4
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 2 test cases, 15 skipped, 4 test suites, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

The test cases of the top suite `Sample` are skipped too, since its name
does not match the first pattern.
